Magpie model gateway

Install:
  Install the missing Magpie terminal build through the official installer and its release checksum verification.
  Install and start its systemd user service with the gateway enabled and the Web interface disabled.
  Enable user lingering so the service starts at boot and continues after logout.

Upgrade:
  Skip the operation when the managed Magpie executable is not installed.
  Install the current terminal release and refresh the gateway service with the current shell environment.
  Enable service startup and user lingering.

Status:
  Report the managed executable version, daemon state, gateway availability and user lingering.
  Query local state without installing or configuring the application.

Requirements:
  Ubuntu on amd64 or arm64, APT and a regular user with sudo access.
  A running systemd instance and an accessible systemd user manager; WSL must have systemd enabled.
  Installation and upgrades require access to usemagpie.ai and its release download sources.

Configuration:
  Executable: ~/.local/bin/magpie
  User service: ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/systemd/user/magpie.service
  Use magpie daemon install --no-web to generate and enable the upstream service; existing executable and service files are backed up before replacement.
  The service inherits supported environment settings, including PATH and proxy variables, from the invoking shell.
  Enable lingering for the current user through loginctl; this keeps the user's enabled services available without an active login.
  The gateway uses Magpie's configuration, with 127.0.0.1:3425 as the upstream default.
  Run magpie tui or magpie web to manage providers and models; the Web interface runs only when requested.
  Magpie manages its own settings, accounts and updates. The module does not select providers, enter keys or change agent models.
